FIN and ACK flags are normally used to close a connection gracefully.

### 3\. How to use and identify TCP flags in Kali Linux or Ubuntu

You can use Wireshark and tcpdump to observe these flags.

A. Using tcpdump

First, identify your network interface:

```bash
ip a
```

Capture TCP packets:

```bash
sudo tcpdump -i any -nn tcp
```

Display only TCP reset packets:

```bash
sudo tcpdump -i any -nn 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-rst != 0'
```

Display SYN packets:

```bash
sudo tcpdump -i any -nn 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0'
```

Display FIN packets:

```bash
sudo tcpdump -i any -nn 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-fin != 0'
```

B. Using Wireshark

Open Wireshark and enter these display filters:

| Filter               | What it displays |
| -------------------- | ---------------- |
| tcp.flags.syn == 1   | SYN packets      |
| tcp.flags.ack == 1   | ACK packets      |
| tcp.flags.fin == 1   | FIN packets      |
| tcp.flags.reset == 1 | RST packets      |
| tcp.flags.push == 1  | PSH packets      |
| tcp.flags.urg == 1   | URG packets      |
| tcp.flags.ece == 1   | ECE packets      |
| tcp.flags.cwr == 1   | CWR packets      |

### 4\. Important differences for your examination

| TCP flag | Main function                       |
| -------- | ----------------------------------- |
| SYN      | Starts connection                   |
| ACK      | Acknowledges receipt                |
| FIN      | Gracefully finishes connection      |
| RST      | Immediately resets connection       |
| PSH      | Requests prompt data delivery       |
| URG      | Indicates urgent pointer is valid   |
| ECE      | Signals ECN congestion information  |
| CWR      | Signals congestion window reduction |
| NS       | ECN-related protection flag         |

Exam tip

Remember these four especially:

* SYN = Start.

* ACK = Acknowledge.

* FIN = Finish.

* RST = Reset.

